## Amendment, after the exclusive-handoff terms: the collector needed a wider list

The terms recorded for Codex's handoff say **"any file that reaches `mlx` or `mlx_lm` at any
scope, not only the module-scope pinned set"**. The first version of this patch keyed the collector
on `TESTS_THAT_LOAD_MLX`, which is the module-scope set, and that is **five files short**:

| file | why the narrower list misses it |
| --- | --- |
| `test_adapter_delta.py` | imports mlx inside a function or fixture |
| `test_cache_equivalence.py` | the same |
| `test_metal_cache_limit.py` | the same |
| `test_runner.py` | the same |
| `test_state_probe.py` | the same |

A function-local `import mlx` maps the library exactly as surely when the test runs. A collector
keyed on the narrower list would have let those five run inside somebody else's window and refused
that seat's launch, which is the failure the window exists to stop.

So there are two pinned lists answering two questions, and both are checked against their own AST
walk. `TESTS_THAT_LOAD_MLX` answers **what maps MLX on import** — the closure walker's question,
and what a person needs before running a suite beside a live model. `TESTS_THAT_CAN_REACH_MLX`
answers **what can map MLX at all**, and it is the one the collector uses. A test asserts the first
is a strict subset of the second, so the two cannot collapse into one list computed from one
question.

This is the same distinction the records rule already draws (issue 89): the closure walker ignores
function-local imports on purpose, and the record guard counts them, because one asks what runs on
import and the other asks what running the file can reach.

### One consequence, recorded rather than worked around

`conftest.py` now contains the words `import mlx` in the comment explaining the any-scope set, so
the grep counter-example test's `held_but_clean` list gains it. The pin is updated deliberately and
the reason is in the test: **the grep holds a file for talking about the thing.** That is the
failure mode a substring rule always has and a closure never does, so the disagreement it
documents is stronger than before rather than weaker.

